/**

@page page_exahype_programmers_guide_new_solver Introduce a new numerical scheme

\tableofcontents

This page walks through what it takes to add a genuinely new solver to
ExaHyPE 2, i.e. a new discretisation (or a new variant of an existing one).
Modelling the
***what*** of a PDE (unknowns, PDE terms) against an existing solver is covered
on the @ref page_exahype_modelling_pde_solver "modelling the PDE solver" page
and does not require anything below. This page is for the ***how*** this continuous
description is translated into code using a numerical scheme.


## Context

An exahype2.api.Solver only holds the descriptive, discretisation-independent
part of a solver (name, unknowns, auxiliary variables, PDE terms). It does
not know how to turn that description into C++. That is delegated to four
abstract building blocks, all nested classes of exahype2.api.Solver
(`src/exahype2/api/Solver.py`), each of which a concrete discretisation has
to subclass and assign an instance of onto the solver:

- **Solver.NumericalScheme** - the actual discretisation. 
- **Solver.GridConstruction** - how the mesh this solver runs on is built and
  kept up to date. Implements `construct_output(exahype2_project,
  peano_project, setup_simulation)`, adding tasks into the setup-phase task
  graph. Assigned via `solver.grid_construction`.
- **Solver.Plotter** - how the solution is written out. A solver can have
  an arbitrary number of plotters, added via `solver.add_plotter()`, e.g. a
  VTK plotter for visualisation and a checkpoint. Each plotter decides
  itself when it is active (@ref exahype2.api.Solver.Solver.Plotter).

We focus on the first aspect here. 

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
<b> Pattern </b>: Each solver has to be tied to three generators (for the numerics, 
the grid/simulation construction and the plotting. Later on, ExaHyPE invokes
construct_output() on each of these guys. They don't know of each other. However,
the numerical scheme can add attributes to the solver once it is attached to the
object in its constructor (that is before anybody calls construct_output()) and 
deposit information there. For example, Finite Volume solvers typicall add information
on patch sizes to the baseline solver, and they also attach the core data storage
(patch objects) to the solver. 
</div>


