# Determine what grid data your solver needs

Every solver needs certain data.
Add patches for example to solver.


Adding these data to the underlying solver object is enough to allow other generators
to see this augmentation. It does however not mean that anything actually happens.
We still have to ensure that the generated Peano project also creates these data.
This is typically done within construct_output():

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
peano_project.datamodel.add_cell(self._solver._patch)
peano_project.datamodel.add_face(self._solver._patch_overlap_current_time_step)
peano_project.datamodel.add_face(self._solver._patch_overlap_update)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

is an example from @ref exahype.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler.__add_data_to_repository()
which I would recommend to study.
Not only does it add the data to the repository.
It also configures the data such that we tweak the realisation of the data structures.
For example, it ensures that the data goes to the heap and does not move over stacks
for the big double fields.
It changes the storage scheme.


