# The four building blocks

- **Solver.SolverGenerator** - turns the solver description into the
  Abstract*.h/.cpp glue classes (`construct_output(exahype2_project,
  peano_project)`). Solvers typically reuse the generic implementation rather
  than provide their own.

`Solver.generate_solver_realisation()` asserts that `grid_construction` and
`numerical_scheme` are set before it calls into any of them, so both are
mandatory; plotters are optional.


# Build chains of single action set tasks

When your numerical scheme needs several action sets in a row, e.g. to reconstruct
a patch with its halo, interpolate the hanging faces and evaluate the
right-hand side, create one task per action set and let each depend on its
predecessor. Do not pack the action sets into one combined task.
The Runge-Kutta scheme @ref exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta
does it this way.

## Why

The task graph compiler @ref peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler.FuseSteps
fuses independent tasks of one dependency layer into a node, and it marks the
action sets of a node as parallel (they may then be invoked concurrently by
the traversal) only if every task in the node holds exactly one action
set. A task with several action sets is a sequence, and the compiler cannot
express a sequence as a parallel strand next to another task. It fuses the
tasks into the same traversal, but invokes their action sets one after the other.

This is irrelevant as long as a project hosts a single solver. It matters as soon as
solvers share a task graph, which is the entire point of
@ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling "coupling":
With super-tasks, the reconstruction of solver A and the reconstruction of
solver B end up as a sequence in the same traversal. With chains, they
form an `a`/`b` pair per layer and overlap.
The layers are numbered by the depth in the dependency graph. If chains
differ in length, an action set of one solver pairs with a different action set of the
other one. This is still parallel.

@dot
digraph G {
  rankdir=LR; node [shape=box, fontsize=10];
  subgraph cluster_a { label="Solver A"; R1 [label="Reconstruct"]; I1 [label="Interpolate"]; E1 [label="Evaluate RHS"]; R1 -> I1 -> E1; }
  subgraph cluster_b { label="Solver B"; R2 [label="Reconstruct"]; I2 [label="Interpolate"]; E2 [label="Evaluate RHS"]; R2 -> I2 -> E2; }
  { rank=same; R1; R2 }
  { rank=same; I1; I2 }
  { rank=same; E1; E2 }
}
@enddot

The chains of two solvers. Each row is one dependency layer. Its action sets run in parallel.

## What you have to take care of

- **Data qualifiers.** If the action sets share cell data, the
  first task keeps the load part of the original qualifier, the last task keeps
  the store part, and all tasks in between load and store.
  The fused traversal merges the qualifiers back into the original one, and a compiler that
  does not fuse still hands the data on.
  @ref exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta provides a helper for this.
- **Face data that a chain shares.** Do not let several tasks of a chain write it:
  The compiler treats a writer as conflicting with every other user of the same face data within one
  traversal and would not fuse the tasks, i.e. you pay additional mesh sweeps.
- **Temporary data rely on fusion.** If the tasks of a chain share data that is declared
  temporary, e.g. the projected face data of the Runge-Kutta scheme, the compiler has to
  fuse the tasks into one traversal. Otherwise, the data are lost between the traversals.
  We learned this the hard way: When only the last task of the projection chain
  (the roll over) declared that it writes the faces of the current time step,
  the projection and the restriction did not conflict with the reconstruction of the same
  stage, which reads these faces. The compiler fused them backwards into the previous traversal and
  only the roll over started a new one. It read an uninitialised update patch, and the next
  stage started with NaNs. The remedy is to declare the persistent data on the first task of
  the chain, so it conflicts with the reader before it, and to declare it as temporary
  on the others. The merged qualifier is the one of the first task.
  Check the compiled graph of your scheme (log it with the text verbosity of the
  generator script) whether all tasks of a chain end up in one traversal.
- **Names of the tasks.** The compiler turns them into the names of C++ enumerators.
  It splits them at everything that is not allowed in an identifier, i.e. you
  can use hyphens and the like. Give the first task of a group the name that other
  code looks it up with, and describe extra tasks by what they do.

# Reference implementation to copy from

Rather than starting from a blank page, base a new discretisation on the
Finite Volumes / Rusanov implementation under `src/exahype2/api/fv/`, which
exercises all four building blocks against the task-graph architecture:

- `RusanovWithExplicitEuler.py` - Solver.NumericalScheme. Creates the patch
  and patch-overlap mesh data in its constructor (@see
  `__create_data_structures()`), wires up the storage generators and mesh
  data repository (@see `__add_data_to_repository()`), and assembles the
  time-stepping task graph (reconstruction, cell update, CFL condition,
  project on faces, compute time step size).
- `SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid.py` / `SetupSimulationWithUserCodedRefinementCriterion.py`
  - two Solver.GridConstruction variants: a mesh that is refined once and
  then stays regular, versus one refined adaptively every sweep by a
  user-coded criterion.
- `PlotAsVTKFile.py` - Solver.Plotter, maps the patch data onto a plot mesh
  and writes it as VTK. It can be restricted to some of the quantities and
  to boxes of the domain.
- `WriteCheckpoint.py` - Solver.Plotter, reconstructs the patches' halos and
  writes the patches with halo plus the solver's state without loss.
- `../blockstructured/LoadCheckpoint.py` - Solver.GridConstruction which
  replaces the setup over a regular grid: it rebuilds the mesh of a
  checkpoint, loads the patches with halo and restores the solver's state.

A new discretisation typically needs its own patch/data layout and its own
task graph in the Solver.NumericalScheme subclass, but can often reuse the
existing Solver.GridConstruction and Solver.Plotter variants unchanged if its
data still fits the same patch-based layout.

The fourth-order Finite Differences under `src/exahype2/api/rkfd4/` are a
second example. They follow the same structure, but are fully independent of
`src/exahype2/api/fv/`: grid construction, plotter and action sets are copies
adapted to a halo of three grid cells. Their numerical scheme
(`CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta.py`) shows how a scheme with several
stages per time step builds its task graph, and how it combines kernels
written in DSLHyPE (`FD4Kernels.py`) with the discretisation-independent
Runge-Kutta tools from `src/exahype2/api/rungekutta/`.


# Wiring it into a project

The user-facing Python script that exercises a solver assigns the three
building blocks and hands the solver to an exahype2.api.Project, e.g. (see
`benchmarks/exahype2/euler/smooth-convergence-test/smooth-convergence-test.py`
for the full, working example):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
thesolver = exahype2.api.Solver(solver_name, unknowns, auxiliary_variables)
thesolver.add_pde_term(exahype2.api.Flux(stateless=False))
thesolver.add_pde_term(exahype2.api.MaxEigenvalue(stateless=False))

thesolver.numerical_scheme = exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler(thesolver, patch_size)
thesolver.grid_construction = exahype2.api.fv.SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid(thesolver, volume_size=...)
thesolver.add_plotter(exahype2.api.fv.PlotAsVTKFile(thesolver))
thesolver.add_plotter(exahype2.api.fv.WriteCheckpoint(thesolver, time_in_between_plots=1.0))

project.add_solver(thesolver)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

`project.generate_Peano4_project()` then calls
`Solver.generate_solver_realisation()` on every registered solver, which in
turn calls each building block's `construct_output()` in sequence
(`grid_construction`, then `numerical_scheme`, then all plotters, then
`mesh_adaptation`, and last the `solver_generator`, so all of them can add
members to the solver's C++ class first).

